### ECFT: identify the traded and invoice currencies

The **Euro Currency Futures Tutorial (ECFT)** trades EUR 25,000 per contract against the US dollar. The euro is the underlying/traded/base currency. The dollar is the invoice/variable currency. A quotation of $1.2311\ \mathrm{USD/EUR}$ means that one euro costs 1.2311 dollars. The long receives euros and pays dollars at delivery; the short delivers euros and receives the dollar amount. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Financial_Markets_Instruments_I/Week_2/L11(ExamplesOfFinancialFutures).pptx|L11(ExamplesOfFinancialFutures).pptx, slide 5]]

One tick is USD 0.0001 per euro, which the source describes as one hundredth of a US cent per euro. Its monetary value is

$$
25{,}000\ \mathrm{EUR}\times0.0001\ \mathrm{USD/EUR}=\text{USD }2.50.
$$

The EUR units cancel. Initial margin is USD 1,000, about 3.5% of the contract value when the exchange rate is 1.1345 USD/EUR. Delivery months are the same four quarterly months. The last trading day is the third Wednesday of the delivery month at 10:30 a.m.; delivery is the next business day. EDSP averages the rates of 15 reference banks at 10:30 a.m., excluding the three highest and three lowest. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Financial_Markets_Instruments_I/Week_2/L11(ExamplesOfFinancialFutures).pptx|L11(ExamplesOfFinancialFutures).pptx, slide 5]]

![[01_Notes/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Financial_Markets_Instruments_I/assets/Week_2_L11_Currency_Specification.png]]

*The currency specification keeps quotation units, tick size and margin currency distinct. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Financial_Markets_Instruments_I/Week_2/L11(ExamplesOfFinancialFutures).pptx|L11(ExamplesOfFinancialFutures).pptx, slide 5]]*

#### Closing after ten days

The long buys ten contracts at $F_0=1.2311$ and closes at $F_{10}=1.2383$. The rise is $0.0072/0.0001=72$ ticks, giving

$$
\Pi=10(72)(2.5)=\text{USD }1{,}800.
$$

The margin return is $1800/(10\times1000)=18\%$. Simple annual scaling yields $0.18\times365/10=6.57=657\%$. The gain is in dollars even though the underlying contract size is in euros. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Financial_Markets_Instruments_I/Week_2/L11(ExamplesOfFinancialFutures).pptx|L11(ExamplesOfFinancialFutures).pptx, slide 6]]

#### Physical delivery fixes the effective purchase rate

If the ten contracts remain open until delivery, the long receives EUR 250,000. At EDSP $1.2401$, the invoice is $250000\times1.2401=\text{USD }310{,}025$. The opening-to-delivery gain is $10(2401-2311)(2.5)=\text{USD }2{,}250$. Combining the invoice and the futures gain gives

$$
\frac{310{,}025-2{,}250}{250{,}000}=1.2311\ \mathrm{USD/EUR}=F_0.
$$

The long pays the EDSP invoice but has already received offsetting futures gains. This is why the effective purchase price reproduces the opening futures rate in the source's matched delivery example. The ten-day USD 1,800 gain and the delivery USD 2,250 gain describe different exit times and must not be added together. The calculation leaves funding timing and transaction costs outside the illustration. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Financial_Markets_Instruments_I/Week_2/L11(ExamplesOfFinancialFutures).pptx|L11(ExamplesOfFinancialFutures).pptx, slide 6]]

![[01_Notes/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Financial_Markets_Instruments_I/assets/Week_2_L11_Currency_Closing_and_Delivery.png]]

*The source currency timeline and invoice calculation show the alternative closing and delivery paths. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Financial_Markets_Instruments_I/Week_2/L11(ExamplesOfFinancialFutures).pptx|L11(ExamplesOfFinancialFutures).pptx, slide 6]]*


## Worked example

O010-W1 / item_version 1, worked_example. **Prompt:** Hypothetical ECFT: EUR25000 per contract; quote USD per EUR; tick0.0001, initial margin USD1000 per contract. To buy EUR later you go long 10 at 1.2311. Alternative A closes at 1.2383 after 10 days; alternative B holds to physical delivery with EDSP 1.2401. State base/invoice currencies, compute tick value, A P/L and margin return (also simple365-day annualisation), B invoice and cumulative futures gain, net USD purchase cost and effective USD/EUR. Explain sign, matching and costs assumptions, why the paths cannot be combined, and whether the specification/return proves current terms or an underlying yield.

**Steps and interpretation:** Underlying/base EUR, quote/invoice USD; tick=25000×.0001=USD2.50. Bought amount EUR250000. A G=250000×(1.2383−1.2311)=USD1800.000000; margin USD10000, return 18%, simple annualised 657%. B invoice=250000×1.2401=USD310025.000000; cumulative long G=USD2250.000000. Net invoice−G=USD307775.000000, divided by EUR250000=USD1.2311/EUR. The same quantity/currency and settlement endpoint make the algebra cancel EDSP; funding, fees and basis/contract mismatch are excluded. A closed trade cannot also receive B held gain. Increases in USD/EUR raise physical EUR purchase cost and produce a compensating long gain. Collateral return is not the currency asset yield; hypothetical specifications are not current verified exchange rules.

## Supported completion

O010-S1 / item_version 1, supported_completion. **Prompt:** Hypothetical ECFT: EUR25000 per contract; quote USD per EUR; tick0.0001, initial margin USD1000 per contract. To buy EUR later you go long 2 at 1.2000. Alternative A closes at 1.2020 after 5 days; alternative B holds to physical delivery with EDSP 1.2050. State base/invoice currencies, compute tick value, A P/L and margin return (also simple365-day annualisation), B invoice and cumulative futures gain, net USD purchase cost and effective USD/EUR. Explain sign, matching and costs assumptions, why the paths cannot be combined, and whether the specification/return proves current terms or an underlying yield.

**Key:** Underlying/base EUR, quote/invoice USD; tick=25000×.0001=USD2.50. Bought amount EUR50000. A G=50000×(1.2020−1.2000)=USD100.000000; margin USD2000, return 5%, simple annualised 365%. B invoice=50000×1.2050=USD60250.000000; cumulative long G=USD250.000000. Net invoice−G=USD60000.000000, divided by EUR50000=USD1.2000/EUR. The same quantity/currency and settlement endpoint make the algebra cancel EDSP; funding, fees and basis/contract mismatch are excluded. A closed trade cannot also receive B held gain. Increases in USD/EUR raise physical EUR purchase cost and produce a compensating long gain. Collateral return is not the currency asset yield; hypothetical specifications are not current verified exchange rules.

Allowed support: show the named definition or equation in Motivation and explanation, then ask for the missing substitution and interpretation. At most two hints: first identify the relevant distinction; second point to the first keyed step. Reveal the worked solution if those fail. Supported success does not establish acquisition. Fade assistance only once the learner explains the keyed steps; then hide the solution and use an unused full check.

## Rubric and independence

Pass requires every listed component, all requested outputs and essential keyed reasoning without a critical conceptual error. Algebraically equivalent formulas, explicitly defined opposite cash-flow signs and equivalent plain-language reasoning are accepted. Partial means a missing component or a correct method with an unresolved isolated arithmetic slip; fail means the central mechanism or condition is wrong. Unscorable means the response or help/exposure conditions cannot be established. Unknown conditions never certify independence. Arithmetic slips require independent correction before a pass. No numerical grade is invented.

Critical errors: Inverting USD/EUR without changing cash-flow units; adding gain to purchase cost; using close and delivery simultaneously; claiming a hedge with mismatched quantities or ignoring funding.

Acquisition requires two distinct unassisted passes from I1/I2/D1/D2 using the exact six routes in metadata. Each full prompt and key covers every component; any pair therefore covers the whole outcome twice. A fresh D item used for acquisition cannot later certify delayed retention. Worked/support/prerequisite items and the narrow C1 correction cannot substitute for a full check. The finite bank must not be reused after exposure; request a plan_issue when fewer than two fresh full items remain.

| Component | I1 | I2 | D1 | D2 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| currency_units_and_direction | required | required | required | required |
| close_vs_delivery_cashflows | required | required | required | required |
| effective_rate_and_boundaries | required | required | required | required |

## Misconceptions and correction

An invoice plus a favourable long gain usually signals confusion between cash received and purchase cost. Write invoice as an outflow, gain as an inflow; subtract once, using matching EUR amounts. Observe the learner's reasoning before diagnosing: a sign or omitted word alone is tentative. Score before help. O010-C1 / item_version 1, narrow_correction. **Fresh prompt:** Fresh narrow check: EUR100 invoice at1.40USD/EUR, matched long entry1.35. Net purchase cost? **Key and rubric:** InvoiceUSD140 minus gainUSD5 =USD135, effective1.35USD/EUR; same EUR quantity and no costs assumed. Pass only if the keyed repaired component is supplied; partial for an omission or arithmetic slip; fail for the same conflicting mechanism. C1 is not a full independent check. After two ineffective hints show the relevant worked step; after another failed correction pause and return to the named prerequisite or explanation.

## Immediate and delayed checks

All items are item_version 1 and full_capability_check. I1/I2 are immediate forms; D1/D2 are distinct delayed forms. Delayed success requires an unused unassisted item before recap, at least 24 actual elapsed hours after acquisition and the most recent relevant teaching/solution exposure, with intervening exposure recorded. A date change alone does not qualify. Early D attempts may be acquisition checks only. Each item is a bounded parallel or near-context variant, not proof of broad transfer. Each key states the full required response; do not improvise extra variants.

### O010-I1

**Prompt:** Hypothetical ECFT: EUR25000 per contract; quote USD per EUR; tick0.0001, initial margin USD1000 per contract. To buy EUR later you go long 3 at 1.1000. Alternative A closes at 1.0960 after 12 days; alternative B holds to physical delivery with EDSP 1.0920. State base/invoice currencies, compute tick value, A P/L and margin return (also simple365-day annualisation), B invoice and cumulative futures gain, net USD purchase cost and effective USD/EUR. Explain sign, matching and costs assumptions, why the paths cannot be combined, and whether the specification/return proves current terms or an underlying yield.

**Key and essential rubric:** Underlying/base EUR, quote/invoice USD; tick=25000×.0001=USD2.50. Bought amount EUR75000. A G=75000×(1.0960−1.1000)=USD-300.000000; margin USD3000, return -10%, simple annualised -304.167%. B invoice=75000×1.0920=USD81900.000000; cumulative long G=USD-600.000000. Net invoice−G=USD82500.000000, divided by EUR75000=USD1.1000/EUR. The same quantity/currency and settlement endpoint make the algebra cancel EDSP; funding, fees and basis/contract mismatch are excluded. A closed trade cannot also receive B held gain. Increases in USD/EUR raise physical EUR purchase cost and produce a compensating long gain. Collateral return is not the currency asset yield; hypothetical specifications are not current verified exchange rules. All components are required.

